class.description:
Every `.hx` file in the project is loaded as a [HaxeScript]. The file is compiled by hxcpp into the project library, and the class it declares is attached to a node like any other script. The class extends a Godot class, either directly, through another project Haxe script, or through the generated `godot.*` bindings:
[codeblock lang=haxe]
package game;

import godot.Node2D;

class Player extends Node2D {
	@:export var speed:Float = 200.0;

	override public function _ready():Void {
		position = new godot.Vector2(10, 10);
	}
}
[/codeblock]
Members are exposed to the Inspector with `@:export`, a class runs in the editor with `@:tool`, and a [code]@:signal[/code] function declares a Godot signal. A Haxe script cannot be embedded in a scene or resource: hxcpp compiles it ahead of time, so a script is always a standalone file with a path.
[b]Note:[/b] The script is compiled as part of the project's Haxe library. A script that was only just edited reports its parsed members to the editor, but the new code runs after the library is rebuilt (running the project builds the game library).
